You are helping me build "Workforce 360 ERP" — a production-grade, modular ERP web
application, based on an internal PRD (Workforce 360 ERP PRD v1.0). Treat this as a
long-lived project spanning many sessions. I will give you one phase at a time. Do not
build modules from later phases unless I explicitly ask.

PRODUCT SUMMARY
Workforce 360 ERP centralizes an organization's core operations: recruitment/candidate
onboarding, HR, attendance/leave, asset management, finance/invoicing, payroll, business
development, project management, development & QA support, help desk, document
management, notifications, approval workflows, administration, reporting, and security.
It must be built so advanced features (AI, multi-tenancy, native mobile apps, enterprise
integrations, predictive analytics) can be added later WITHOUT major architectural rework.
Favor modular, decoupled design over shortcuts, even in the MVP.

ARCHITECTURE — HARD RULE
This is a strict two-tier system: a Next.js frontend and a standalone Node.js backend.
- The frontend (/apps/web) NEVER connects to the database, Supabase Admin SDK, or any
  data store directly. It has zero DB credentials, zero service-role keys, and zero
  ORM/DB driver dependencies. Its only source of data is the backend's REST API, called
  through a typed API client.
- The backend (/apps/api) owns ALL database access, ALL business logic, ALL auth
  issuing/verification, and ALL RBAC enforcement. Every write and every sensitive read
  goes through backend middleware (auth check -> RBAC check -> validation -> controller
  -> service -> repository).
- If Cursor ever proposes calling Supabase, Prisma, Postgres, or any DB client from
  inside /apps/web (including Server Components, Server Actions, or Route Handlers),
  stop and flag it — that violates this rule. Server Components in the frontend may call
  the backend API server-side (e.g. for SSR), but must still go through HTTP, not a DB
  driver.

TECH STACK
- Frontend (/apps/web): Next.js 14+ (App Router), TypeScript strict mode, Tailwind CSS
  + shadcn/ui, TanStack Query for all server-state, calling the backend via a typed API
  client. No direct DB access (see ARCHITECTURE above).
- Backend (/apps/api): Node.js + TypeScript, Express (or Fastify), layered as
  routes -> middleware -> controllers -> services -> repositories. Exposes a REST API
  consumed only by the frontend (and later, external integrations).
- Database: PostgreSQL, accessed only from the backend via Prisma (preferred) or Knex.
  Supabase may be used as the hosted Postgres instance and for Storage, but the
  frontend never talks to Supabase directly — the backend proxies everything.
- Auth: Backend-issued JWT access + refresh tokens (httpOnly cookies or an
  Authorization header — decide in Phase 0 and document it). Backend owns password
  hashing, OAuth callback handling, and token verification.
- Validation: Zod, with schemas defined once (shared package if monorepo) and enforced
  as backend middleware on every route — this is the authoritative validation layer.
  Frontend may reuse the schemas for UX but must never be trusted as the source of truth.
- Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) enforced as backend middleware on every route —
  never rely on hiding UI alone, and never enforce it only in the frontend.

TARGET USER ROLES (design permissions around these from day one)
Super Administrator, Administrator, HR Team, Finance Team, Payroll Team,
Business Development Team, Project Managers, Developers, QA Engineers, Team Leads,
Employees, Candidates.

CODING STANDARDS
- Monorepo folder structure:
  /apps/web        (Next.js frontend: /app, /components, /lib, /types)
  /apps/api        (Node.js backend: /src/routes, /src/middleware, /src/controllers,
                     /src/services, /src/repositories, /src/types, /db/migrations)
  /packages/shared (optional: shared Zod schemas / TS types used by both apps)
- Every table has: created_at, updated_at, deleted_at (soft delete — never hard-delete)
- Every write action (create/update/delete/approve) writes an audit log entry
  (actor, action, entity, before/after or diff, timestamp) — written by the backend
  service layer, never by the frontend
- Versioned SQL migrations only (Prisma Migrate or a migrations folder) — never
  hand-edit prod schema
- All API responses use a consistent shape: { data, error, meta }
- All list endpoints support pagination, sorting, and filtering
- TypeScript types/interfaces for every entity, defined in the backend and either
  shared via a package or re-declared in the frontend's /types — but the backend's
  shape is always the source of truth
- Every module must be self-contained enough to be extended or replaced later without
  touching unrelated modules — this is a hard requirement, on both frontend and backend
- Every backend route runs through: auth middleware -> RBAC/permission middleware ->
  Zod validation middleware -> controller -> service -> repository, in that order

NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS (apply to every phase)
- Page load under 3 seconds, API response under 2 seconds
- Encryption in transit (HTTPS everywhere) and at rest; no secrets in client bundles or
  plaintext; no DB credentials or service-role keys ever ship to the frontend
- Daily backups, disaster recovery readiness
- Responsive UI (desktop-first, must work on tablet and mobile browser)
- Role-based dashboards — a user only ever sees data/actions relevant to their role,
  enforced by the backend, not just hidden in the UI

OUT OF SCOPE FOR NOW (do not build unless I explicitly ask later)
AI features (AI assistant, resume ranking, predictive analytics, HR/Finance/Project AI),
native mobile apps, multi-company/multi-tenant support, Microsoft Teams/Slack/GitHub/
GitLab/Jira/Zoom/QuickBooks/Xero/Azure AD/LDAP/SAML integrations, offline support,
international payroll/localization.

WORKING STYLE
- Ask before making irreversible schema decisions if something is ambiguous.
- After finishing a phase, summarize what was built and list TODOs or shortcuts taken.
- Write basic tests for critical logic (RBAC checks, payroll calculations, invoice totals,
  leave balance math) — these live in the backend, next to the service they test.
- Keep commits scoped to one phase/feature at a time.
- If a requirement's exact field list or validation rule isn't given in the phase prompt,
  make a reasonable assumption, state it in your summary, and move on rather than stalling.